There has been absolutely no change in the original population; they still require the same level of adaptation as before.
As time passes, as the new population adapts to its new environment, it gradually begins to resemble other populations.
Eventually, thousands or perhaps millions of years later, these two populations will look so different that they can no longer be considered part of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a very important part of evolution.
